![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Explicitly throwing an error inside a `try` block only to catch it in the immediate `catch` block of the same `try-catch` statement is an anti-pattern. Since `supabase.rpc` returns `{ data, error }` as a resolved value rather than throwing on database-level errors, you can avoid this redundancy by checking `if (error)` outside of the `try-catch` block.

For example:
```javascript
  let result;
  try {
    result = await supabase.rpc('get_evidence_context', {
      p_user_id: userId,
      p_topic_path: topicPath,
      p_limit: limit,
    });
  } catch (err) {
    if (isEvidenceContextRpcUnavailable(err)) {
      return null;
    }
    throw toEvidenceContextRpcError(err);
  }

  const { data, error } = result;
  if (error) {
    if (isEvidenceContextRpcUnavailable(error)) {
      return null;
    }
    throw toEvidenceContextRpcError(error);
  }
```
This separates actual execution/network exceptions from API/database-level error responses and avoids double-processing the error.

